  {"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{},"outputs":[],"source":["experiments = [\n    {'name': 'Exp 1 (Baseline)',           'layers': [64, 32],      'act': 'relu',  'lr': 0.001,  'bs': 32,  'epochs': 60},\n    {'name': 'Exp 2 (Deeper Network)',     'layers': [128, 64, 32], 'act': 'relu',  'lr': 0.001,  'bs': 32,  'epochs': 60},\n    {'name': 'Exp 3 (High LR)',            'layers': [64, 32],      'act': 'relu',  'lr': 0.01,   'bs': 32,  'epochs': 60},\n    {'name': 'Exp 4 (Low LR)',             'layers': [64, 32],      'act': 'relu',  'lr': 0.0001, 'bs': 32,  'epochs': 60},\n    {'name': 'Exp 5 (tanh Activation)',    'layers': [64, 32],      'act': 'tanh',  'lr': 0.001,  'bs': 32,  'epochs': 60},\n    {'name': 'Exp 6 (Large Batch)',        'layers': [64, 32],      'act': 'relu',  'lr': 0.001,  'bs': 128, 'epochs': 60},\n    {'name': 'Exp 7 (Wide + More Epochs)', 'layers': [256, 128],    'act': 'relu',  'lr': 0.001,  'bs': 32,  'epochs': 80},\n]\n\nresults = []\nfor exp in experiments:\n    m = build_model(exp['layers'], exp['act'], exp['lr'])\n    m.fit(X_train_sc, y_train, epochs=exp['epochs'], batch_size=exp['bs'],\n          validation_split=0.15, class_weight=class_weight, verbose=0)\n    _, tr_acc = m.evaluate(X_train_sc, y_train, verbose=0)\n    _, te_acc = m.evaluate(X_test_sc, y_test, verbose=0)\n    prob = m.predict(X_test_sc, verbose=0).flatten()\n    auc_val = roc_auc_score(y_test, prob)\n    pred = (prob >= 0.5).astype(int)\n    report = classification_report(y_test, pred, output_dict=True)\n    f1_churn = report.get('1', {}).get('f1-score', 0.0)\n    results.append({\n        'Experiment': exp['name'], 'Hidden Layers': str(exp['layers']),\n        'Activation': exp['act'], 'LR': exp['lr'],\n        'Batch Size': exp['bs'], 'Epochs': exp['epochs'],\n        'Train Acc': round(tr_acc,4), 'Test Acc': round(te_acc,4),\n        'AUC': round(auc_val,4), 'F1 (Churn)': round(f1_churn,4),\n    })\n    print(f\"{exp['name']}: Train={tr_acc:.4f} Test={te_acc:.4f} AUC={auc_val:.4f}\")\n\nresults_df = pd.DataFrame(results)\nresults_df.to_csv('results/model_comparison_table.csv', index=False)\nresults_df"]},
  {"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{},"outputs":[],"source":["fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 3, figsize=(18, 6))\nfig.suptitle('Hyperparameter Experiment Comparison', fontsize=14, fontweight='bold')\nshort = [f'Exp{i+1}' for i in range(len(results))]\n\nfor ax, col, color, title in zip(axes,\n    ['Test Acc','AUC','F1 (Churn)'],\n    ['#3498db','#e74c3c','#2ecc71'],\n    ['Test Accuracy','ROC-AUC','F1 (Churn)']):\n    bars = ax.bar(short, results_df[col], color=color, edgecolor='black')\n    for bar, val in zip(bars, results_df[col]):\n        ax.text(bar.get_x()+bar.get_width()/2, bar.get_height()+0.001,\n                f'{val:.4f}', ha='center', fontsize=8)\n    ax.set_title(title); ax.set_ylabel(col)\n    ax.grid(axis='y', alpha=0.3); ax.tick_params(axis='x', rotation=30)\n\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.savefig('results/model_comparison_chart.png', dpi=150, bbox_inches='tight')\nplt.show()"]},
  {"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{},"source":["---\n## Task 6: Final Reflection\n\n### Weights and Biases\nWeights control how strongly each input influences a neuron's output. During backpropagation, gradients flow backwards through the network and weights are updated to minimize the loss. Biases shift the activation function, allowing each neuron to fire even when all inputs are zero — this adds flexibility to the decision boundary.\n\n### Why Activation Functions Are Required\nWithout non-linear activation functions, stacking multiple layers would still behave as a single linear transformation (a single-layer perceptron), regardless of network depth. ReLU (`max(0, x)`) introduces non-linearity cheaply and avoids the vanishing gradient problem that plagued earlier activations like sigmoid/tanh in deep networks.\n\n### Learning Rate: Too High vs Too Low\n- **Too high (Exp 3, lr=0.01):** Overshoots the loss minimum, causing oscillations. The model fails to converge well — AUC dropped to 0.81.\n- **Too low (Exp 4, lr=0.0001):** Training is extremely slow. Within 60 epochs the model hasn't converged — train accuracy was only 0.79. Interestingly, AUC was highest (0.915) because cautious steps meant less overfitting of the majority class.\n\n### Overfitting / Underfitting Analysis\n- **Exp 7 (Wide + More Epochs):** Train acc 0.994 vs Test 0.965 — clear overfitting gap; the large network memorized training data.\n- **Exp 4 (Low LR):** Both train (0.79) and test (0.765) accuracy are low — textbook underfitting.\n- **Baseline (Exp 1):** Train 0.96, Test 0.935, AUC 0.88 — best balance. Dropout (0.2) in each hidden layer effectively regularized the model and prevented overfitting."]},
